  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 126 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nA moisture plume looks to remain intact into Monday, stretching \nfrom Sonora and the Gila into northeastern NM, however the overall\nPWAT values will decrease (generally within a 0.8 to 1.1 inch \nrange). The upper high will be continuing to elongate on a west-\neast axis as pressure rises build farther west. This will keep the\narea stretching from the southwestern mountains toward the Sangre\nde Cristos favored for thunderstorm development Monday afternoon \nwith steering flow turning a bit more slow and erratic. \n\nStorm coverage looks to reduce substantially into Tuesday and \nWednesday with just isolated to scattered cells initiating over the \nhigh terrain, mostly from southwestern to northeastern NM. While \nhigh pressure will remain somewhat elongated along a west-east axis \nthrough this Tuesday and Wednesday time frame, it will be building \nmore centrally over NM, causing PWATs to reduce and limit the storm \ncoverage. \n\nThere is potential for the upper high to nudge a bit to the \nnorthwest, becoming more concentric over the Four Corners into west \nTX, and this could open the door to another backdoor front as an \nupper trough moves into the midwest. Northeastern NM zones would be \nthe most obvious beneficiaries of this potential increase in \nmoisture and upslope flow on Thursday before the cold pool could be \nconvectively enhanced and assisted farther south and west. \nHowever, confidence is rather low in the finer details with this \nscenario, given much uncertainty with the larger scale features.",
